# Models (large binary files — store in object storage, not git)
*.pkl
*.joblib
*.h5
models/trained/

# ...except the triage model, which IS committed on purpose.
#
# It is 560KB, it is the artefact the whole Phase 4 pipeline depends on, and
# predict.py's fallback when it is missing is to retrain from
# 03_CLASSIFICATION/splits/train.csv — a file this repo deliberately does not
# carry, because it holds incident data. So a clone without this file yields a
# backend that starts cleanly and then triages nothing: every report saved with
# severity NULL, no error anywhere except one startup warning.
#
# Committing it also pins provenance: app/ml/VERSION records which dataset
# release produced the model that produced a given severity.
!app/ml/07_MODELS/*.joblib

# Whisper weights, fetched by faster-whisper on first load.
#
# NOT committed, unlike the triage model above, and the difference is
# deliberate. The .joblib is 560KB and is the artefact whose provenance the
# repo pins. These are ~500MB per size, are byte-identical for everyone who
# asks CTranslate2 for the same model name, and carry no project provenance:
# app/services/asr_engines.py names the model, requirements.txt pins the
# library, and that is the whole reproducibility story. A clone re-downloads
# them on first startup.
app/ml/asr_models/

# Face-recognition weights for automatic ID/selfie matching.
#
# 13.6 MB of binary, fetched by scripts/fetch_face_model.py rather than
# committed: it is not ours to redistribute (InsightFace publishes buffalo_s
# for non-commercial research use), and a file this size lives in every clone
# and every branch forever once it is in the history.
#
# A deployment without it is a supported state — see face_match_service.
app/ml_models/face/*.onnx
app/ml_models/face/*.part

# Build artefacts served at /download (the Android APK)
public/downloads/
